# exg-c

A local 8-channel EXG host written in C. Plug in a Knight-class board,
watch the traces, clean the line noise, map channels on a cube, save a
profile. Not a medical device. Not affiliated with NeuroPawn.

## Install (once)

```
sudo ./install-usb-permissions.sh
```

Needs libSDL2. The host re-runs itself via `sg dialout` if your user is
not yet in that group.

## Run

```
make
./np-exg
```

Or `./np-exg --cli --port /dev/ttyUSB1 --seconds 8` with no window.

## First session

1. Click **Connect** (or wait — it tries the first USB port on start).
2. Leave the headset **off the head**. Click **NOISE**, then **OK**.
   That is the desk / open-input plate, not EEG.
3. Put the headset on. Sit still. Click **CALM**.
4. Leave **CLN** on. The plot is leftover after that plate + calm DC.
5. Open **Cube**. Each channel sits on a 10-10 cell (Fp1, C3, …) and
   glows in that channel’s color when leftover vs CALM is SIGNAL.
6. Open **Settings**. Type a profile name (for example `motor`) and
   click **Save**. **Load** brings it back later.
7. On Cube or Settings, click **algo …** to pick how each cell becomes
   0 or 1 (detect, sign, mean, energy, delta, fold, proton). Optional.
   Learn Record stores the whole 8³ matrix with that fold.

## What the tabs do

| Tab | Use it for |
|-----|------------|
| **Main** | Live traces, FFT, learn/match, NOISE / CALM / CLEAN |
| **Cube** | 8×8×8 state matrix. Shell = headset sites. Inside = IMU (and plugins). Drag to spin. Click a channel, then a named cell, to move it. |
| **Settings** | UI size, window, channel colors, **Save / Load / next** profiles |

Default montage: ch1–8 → Fp1 Fp2 C3 C4 P3 P4 O1 O2.

## Profiles

A profile is the whole setup: UI scale, window size, notch / high-pass,
CLEAN, gain, colors, channel on/RLD, and electrode sites.

- Last session also autosaves to `~/.config/exg-c.ini`.
- Named files live in `~/.config/exg-c/profiles/<name>.ini`.
- **next** walks saved names; then click **Load**.
- Names: letters, digits, `-`, `_`. Enter in the name box also Saves.

## Keys

`c` connect · `d` disconnect · `r` record CSV · space pause · `1`–`8`
toggle a channel · `q` quit

## Cleaning the line (honest)

NOISE is ~8 s and a Welch plate. CLEAN destroys bins that match that
plate, then the line lock, then calm DC. CALM is worn-still. Leftover
is what Learn can store.

Default plot is ±200 µV. After NOISE, a channel still at that baseline
is drawn **FROZEN** (stops scrolling), like the official host with the
headset off. It moves again when RMS leaves the plate.

60 Hz at 125 SPS is two samples per cycle, so that notch is wide on
purpose. It still cuts line.

USB **keeps streaming** when the analog switches are off. Those
switches only route the front-end. Open inputs sit at the ADS1299 rail
and are labeled open/rail, not EEG.

## Learn

Type a name → **Record 2s** → hold still. MATCH scores live vs saved
poses. Same name overwrites. `del` removes the selected chip. Samples
stay in `~/.config/exg-c.learn`. Learn always runs hp 2 Hz, a 40 Hz
low-pass, and the mains notch.

## Build / test

```
make            # libnplearn.a + ./np-exg
make test       # mock parser, CAL, AUTO, cube, profiles
make test-live  # 5 s collect on /dev/ttyUSB1 (desk, not cortex)
make lib        # matcher only
```

`nplearn/` is a no-heap C library for MCU templates. The host links it.
See `nplearn/README`.

BrainCube plugins include `include/np_cube.h` (`np_virt_claim` /
`np_virt_write`). Interior cells only — the headset shell is reserved.
